---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import meeting from "../../assets/work/articles/data-center.jpg";
import chamber from "../../assets/work/articles/data-center-2.jpg";

const tags = ["Athens-Clarke County", "Mayor and Commissioners", "Data Centers", "Moratorium"];
---

<Base
  title="ACC Commission extends delay on data center moratorium | Grace Joy Park"
  description="Grace Joy Park's meeting story on the Athens-Clarke County commission vote to extend the data center moratorium through June 5."
>
  <p class="back"><a href="/work">&larr; Back to Writing</a></p>

  <article class="post">
    <h1>Athens-Clarke County Commission Extends Delay on Data Center Moratorium</h1>
    <p class="meta muted">Reporting I, Grady College &middot; March 4, 2026 &middot; By Grace Park</p>

    <ul class="tags">
      {tags.map((t) => <li>{t}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <figure class="hero">
      <Image
        src={meeting}
        alt="Community members at the Athens-Clarke County commission meeting"
        widths={[600, 900, 1200, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 900px"
        format="webp"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Community members attend the Athens-Clarke County Mayor and Commission meeting at
        City Hall on March 3, 2026, in Athens, Ga. The meeting included discussion of
        data centers, and commissioners later voted to keep the temporary pause on new
        proposals in place through June 5. (Photo/Grace Park)
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        The Athens-Clarke County mayor and commissioners voted Tuesday at the regular
        session commission meeting. They decided to extend a temporary pause on new data
        centers through June 5, keeping new project proposals on hold after residents
        expressed concerns about the increase in utility costs and strain on local water
        and power systems.
      </p>
      <p>
        The vote came March 3 at City Hall during the regular voting meeting. The pause
        on new data centers started in December, and the March 3 vote pushed that deadline
        back to early June.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tawana Mattox, who holds a doctorate in higher education leadership and is an
        Athens resident, urged county commissioners to reject new data centers in Athens.
      </p>
      <p>
        &ldquo;No more data centers in our Black and working-class neighborhoods,&rdquo;
        Mattox said. &ldquo;We cannot trade our health, our water, our air, our
        neighborhoods for corporate profit.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        In an interview, District 9 Commissioner Ovita Thornton said she initially saw the
        data center in her district as just a warehouse, but later grew concerned about
        what another facility could mean for nearby residents.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="secondary">
      <Image
        src={chamber}
        alt="Athens-Clarke County commission chamber"
        widths={[600, 900, 1200, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 900px"
        format="webp"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Commissioners&rsquo; seats sit inside the Athens-Clarke County commission chamber
        at City Hall on March 3, 2026, in Athens, Ga. The chamber is where commissioners
        vote on council topics. (Photo/Grace Park)
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="full-link">
      <a href="/work/data-center-moratorium.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Read the full story (PDF) &rarr;
      </a>
    </p>

    <section class="why-section">
      <h2>Why I Wrote The Story</h2>
      <p>
        The commission&rsquo;s vote to extend the moratorium through June 5 was the last
        window for residents to push back before any new data center proposals can move,
        which is why the room was full of people raising utility, water and
        environmental-justice concerns. Covering the meeting taught me to track a vote
        alongside several threads of public comment and condense a long agenda into a
        clean lede with the stakes on top. It also pointed me to follow-up sources like
        Commissioner Ovita Thornton and resident Olivia Asher, who I can return to before
        the deadline.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .back { margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 0.95rem; }

  .post h1 {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  .meta {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .tags {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  .tags li {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .hero, .secondary {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .secondary {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .hero :global(img),
  .secondary :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .hero figcaption,
  .secondary figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.5;
    max-width: 65ch;
  }

  .prose p {
    max-width: 65ch;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .prose .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  .full-link {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2.5rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .why-section {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    max-width: 65ch;
  }

  .why-section h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  .why-section p {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
